A variable is a labelled box that holds a value. Use const when the value will never be reassigned, and let when it will change. Start with const by default and only switch to let when you need to reassign. var is the old way and has weird scope rules, which is why we do not use it.

```js
const name = "Sara";
let score = 0;
score = score + 10;
```

Trying to reassign a const throws "TypeError: Assignment to constant variable."

```js
for (let i = 0; i < scores.length; i++) {
  console.log(scores[i]);
}
```

Three parts: start (let i = 0), condition (i < scores.length), step (i++). The loop runs while the condition is true.

When you only need the values, not the index, for...of is simpler and cannot go off by one:

```js
for (const score of scores) {
  total += score;
}
```

A function is a reusable block of code with a name. It takes inputs (parameters) and can give back an output with return.

```js
function calculateAverage(scores) {
  let total = 0;
  for (const score of scores) total += score;
  return total / scores.length;
}
```

Calling it: calculateAverage([80, 90, 100]) gives 90.
